---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Page
  title="Terms of use"
  description="What using MathemAct means, what you agree to when you post, and what the site can and cannot offer."
  lead="Short, because a legal wall nobody reads does not protect anyone either."
  updated="August 2026"
>
  <h2>What this site is</h2>

  <p>
    MathemAct is a community record of how AI tools are used in mathematical research. It is
    a personal project, not the product of any institution. Reading it requires no account.
    Contributing — submitting a report, commenting, answering a claim, or suggesting a
    entry — requires one.
  </p>

  <h2>Content licence</h2>

  <p>
    Everything you submit is published under the{' '}
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="license">
      Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC BY 4.0)
    </a>. By submitting, you confirm that you hold the rights to the content and that you agree
    to license it under those terms. The short version: anyone may share, adapt, and build on
    what you post, provided they say where it came from. See the{' '}
    <a href={path('/licence/')}>licence page</a> for what attribution means in practice.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not paste unpublished work by other people — referee reports, private correspondence,
    third-party conversation transcripts — without their permission. The submission form
    asks for a confirmation that you have removed any such material. Omitting that
    confirmation to proceed is a violation of these terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Conduct</h2>

  <p>
    The <a href={path('/code-of-conduct/')}>code of conduct</a> is part of these terms.
    Posting content that violates it may result in the post being hidden, the account being
    suspended, or both, with no warning where the violation is severe enough.
  </p>

  <h2>Accuracy</h2>

  <p>
    Submissions describe what happened. The verification field — what you checked, not just
    that you checked — is required for a reason: this is a scientific record, and an
    unverified claim is less useful than an honest account of failure. Deliberately false
    submissions are removed.
  </p>

  <h2>The moderation process</h2>

  <p>
    What you post is published immediately. Nobody reviews it first, and nothing in the
    corpus should be read as approved by anyone: it is a record of what people say they did.
  </p>

  <p>
    Moderation is what happens when a reader flags something. A moderator then decides
    whether it stays up or is hidden, and must give a reason for that decision in writing.
    The reason is shown to the author of the content and to the person who flagged it, on
    their account pages. Every decision is also recorded in an audit log. Moderators are
    volunteers; there is no SLA on the time from a flag to a decision. You can see the state
    of your own submissions, and every decision about them, on your account page.
  </p>

  <p>
    A report can be corrected by its author until somebody else has confirmed or commented
    on it, and again while it is hidden. After that its text is fixed, because other people's
    confirmations attest to a version of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Availability</h2>

  <p>
    This is a project maintained on a zero budget. There is no uptime guarantee. The reading
    path — browsing reports, claims, and entries — is served from static files and
    is resilient to the underlying database going offline. The writing path — sign in,
    submit, comment — depends on Supabase's free tier, which may pause for inactivity,
    impose quotas, or become unavailable. The nightly export means the corpus survives even
    if the database does not.
  </p>

  <h2>Limitation of liability</h2>

  <p>
    MathemAct is provided as-is, with no warranty of any kind. The data controller is an
    individual, not an institution. Nothing here constitutes professional advice of any kind.
    The site is not liable for the accuracy of content submitted by third parties, for
    losses arising from site downtime, or for decisions made on the basis of the corpus.
  </p>

  <h2>Governing law</h2>

  <p>
    These terms are governed by the laws of Italy. If you have a dispute that cannot be
    resolved by writing to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>, Italian courts have
    jurisdiction.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>

  <p>
    Material changes to these terms will be announced to registered users before they take
    effect. The full history is in the{' '}
    <a href={SITE.repository}>public repository</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
